title: 'Enum type mismatches are not diagnosed: assigning one enum type to another is silent'
updated_at: "2026-08-30T17:39:22Z"
---
Found 2026-08-31 while verifying that the B056 canonicalization fix could not
move a diagnostic. It cannot - but the reason turned out to be broader than
expected, and worth its own ticket.

## Actual Behavior

`openplanet-lsp check` is SILENT on all of these:

```angelscript
void Mismatch() {
    // two different engine enums
    EDummyCollectionIdent a = EDummyCollectionIdent::Undefined;
    EGhostNameLogoType b = a;              // silent

    // two workspace-declared enums
    LocalOne c = LocalOne::X;
    LocalTwo d = c;                        // silent
}
enum LocalOne { X }
enum LocalTwo { Y }
```

Zero diagnostics. The file IS being analysed - a positive control in the same
file (`Meta::ZZZNoSuchEnumName` as a type, and a wrong-arity call) is flagged
normally, so this is not a parse or resolution failure.

Also silent, and probably the same root: a bad enum VALUE.

```angelscript
EDummyCollectionIdent a = EDummyCollectionIdent::NoSuchValueZZZ;   // silent
```

## Expected Behavior

UNVERIFIED - the game was down when this was found, so there is no live
compiler verdict here. AngelScript does implicitly convert enum values to
integer types, so it is genuinely possible that some or all of the above are
LEGAL and the LSP is correct to stay silent. That must be probed before any
fix. Specifically worth probing separately:

- enum A -> enum B assignment (the case above)
- enum -> int / uint assignment (likely legal)
- int -> enum assignment (likely requires a cast)
- an undeclared value on a known enum (`E::NoSuchValue`)

A game REJECT on any of these makes it a real missing diagnostic. A game
ACCEPT closes that row and is worth recording either way, since right now the
behaviour is simply unknown.

## Why this was found

B056's canonicalization fix changes `canonicalize_type_name` for 57 enums.
`named_types_equivalent` (`src/typecheck/checker.rs`) compares canonicalized
names, so the fix looked like it could move an argument-type diagnostic. It
cannot - measured on a purpose-built plugin covering locals, parameters,
return types, arrays and both scope qualifiers, base vs branch binaries, zero
difference. The reason is that no enum-mismatch diagnostic exists to move.

That makes the B056 fix safe on stronger grounds than the call-graph argument
originally given for it, and it means `named_types_equivalent`'s enum handling
is currently reachable only for unique EXTERNAL overload sets.

